Hi Stu:

The following is a vectorized solution to what (I think) you want,
with the exception that I did not append the tid portion to the output
string. Instead, since tid is a grouping variable, it is output by
default. You can always paste the columns together if you want a
single string in the end.

putString <- function(d, ...)
{
   # Initialize a vector of length(item.string)
   # consisting of single spaces
    s0 <- rep(" ", length(item.string))

   # Find which item codes match elements of item.string
    ind0 <- which(d$item.code %in% item.string)
   # Find which elements of item.string do not match item.code
    ind1 <- which(!(item.string %in% d$item.code))

   # Insert valid responses into s0
    s0[-ind1] <- d$resp[ind0]

   Return a data frame (dplyr::do() likes this :)
    data.frame(s = paste(s0, collapse = ""))
}

suppressPackageStartupMessages(library(dplyr))
DF %>% group_by(tid) %>% do(putString(.))

Output for this example:

Source: local data frame [2 x 2]
Groups: tid [2]

    tid     s
  <int> <chr>
1     1  01 1
2     2  10 1

Assuming this works as you intended, Hadley can stop crying now :)

@Pete: The purpose of the dplyr package is to replace plyr::ddply()
with much faster and more efficient code (as well as more readable
code), so it makes Hadley cry when you use plyr::ddply() in a dplyr
pipeline.  The function dplyr::do() is the appropriate replacement for
the function argument in ddply() [the argument of do() is the function
to be applied to each split of the data indicated by the levels of the
group_by variable(s), hence the use of the placeholder '.'], and much
like plyr, you have to learn how to supply the appropriate inputs and
outputs that make your function work. Typically, you want to supply a
data frame as input and return a data frame object.

Dennis

